re less about, um, killing anything big or I don't, I could care less about story. Like, you know, someone sends a photo of, oh, look at this giant buck or bull I killed. I could, I could really care less. Um, what I am interested in is like the stories and the adventure. Um, so it's like, what, what went into killing that fricking 200 inch buck? You know, like, is it, uh, you just go out on opening day and have a super easy stock with your bow and shoot it or a rifle hunt or whatever. And here's the photo of the animal. Like, I just, I'm completely numb to it. I'm like, I don't, okay, cool. Great. Um, but if you have like this adventure, the, in the story and this memory made, that's what I resonate with. Um, so maybe for this guy, he needs to reevaluate like why, um, I think I've kind of talked about this, like with, in relation to the kids, like, why am I hunting? Um, what are the things that I enjoy about it? Um, and for me, it's either, it really just falls into like going out. I mean, obviously just, just love being, and I was, um, completely and utterly reminded of this Alaska because it was so busy and stressful in August. And then the second we got out there, uh, not the second, took a couple of days to like, just be present in the mountains and just stop thinking about work and everything going on. Um, but man, I mean, there's something certainly, um, cathartic and, and just like, you know, it's like going to church when you're out there, man. Like you're just, it's just peaceful, um, refreshing, resetting, come back to regular life with renewed energy. Um, but also I'm out there for adventure, uh, and, and to make memories. Um, and I'm out there to bring meat home to the family. Uh, so those are, those are my why, you know, my reasons why I'm hunting. And maybe for this guy, he just needs to reevaluate that. Maybe like he might be more where I'm at. Like, I don't care if this buck is 180 or 220. I really, really, really don't care. Um, and I know that's, it's a, um, obviously that's trophy hunting is looked at in a lot of different ways, but in general speaking, like, you know, people want to go shoot big animals. Uh, and it's cool. Like Bosma killed a giant caribou in Alaska. And I was like, it's much more impressive animal than the small one I shot. Um, it's cool. It's going to look cool on the wall. Um, but if that's your only metric for hunting, you're, you're doing it wrong. And there's good, I'm not saying I'm anti-trophy hunting. There are people who are like truly like want the challenge of that. Um, right. Like you, you go on a, who did we just, oh, you were telling, you were telling me the story of the sheep hunter and they, he killed on day one. Right. And you're kind of like that, whenever that happens, especially on a big adventure hunt, there's almost a level of disappointment. Like, oh, that was easier than I thought. You know, you know, kind of like you've, you've killed the animal. So if there's this feeling of disappointment, obviously there's more than you want more out of it than just killing the animal. Um, and yeah, it's just important for everybody to identify that for themselves, what, what they want out of it. And is it, if they truly just, all they care about is a big rack and they want to come by it as easily as possible. To me, that's like, yeah. You know, um, if you want to kill the idea of killing a giant mule deer is cause that buck is wise, old, you know, like just the smart SOBs of a avoided hunters for 10 years of his life. Um, then, uh, then I can get on board with that. Um, yeah, that's good stuff. That echoes a lot. I had thought about that question just this morning before we hopped on and made a couple notes. And a lot of what I was thinking was actually just in line with what you said is like, first of all, what changed? Not just changing how you feel about hunting, but did something else change in your life? Right. Is it, as you said, is it kids and family? Is there some sort